[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001784","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001784","source_record_id":"rec_001784","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Self-supervised learning on millions of pre-mRNA sequences improves sequence-based RNA splicing prediction","abstract":"RNA splicing is an important post-transcriptional process of gene expression in eukaryotic organisms. Here, we developed a novel language model, SpliceBERT, pre-trained on the precursor messenger RNA sequences of 72 vertebrates to improve sequence-based modelling of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT is capable of generating embeddings that preserve the evolutionary information of nucleotides and functional characteristics of splice sites. Moreover, the pre-trained model can be utilized to prioritize potential splice-disrupting variants in an unsupervised manner based on genetic variants' impact on the output of SpliceBERT for sequence context. Benchmarked on a multi-species splice site and a human branchpoint prediction task, SpliceBERT outperformed not only conventional baseline models but also other language models pretrained only on the human genome. Our study highlighted the importance of unsupervised learning with genomic sequences of multiple species and indicated that language models were promising approaches to decipher the determinants of RNA splicing.","doi":"10.1101/2023.01.31.526427","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: Self-supervised learning on millions of pre-mRNA sequences improves sequence-based RNA splicing prediction > TABLE AND FIGURES LEGENDS]\nFigure 1\n\nFigure 1. An overview of SpliceBERT. (A) SpliceBERT was pre-trained on the pre-mRNA sequences of 72 vertebrates under the masked language  modeling  (MLM)  framework.  We  additionally  applied  SpliceBERT  for  downstream  tasks  of  (a)  unsupervised  variant  effect prediction, (b) visualization of nucleotide embeddings, (c) cross-species splice sites prediction and (d) branchpoints prediction and variant analysis. (B) The accuracy of masked language modelling (MLM ACC) of SpliceBERT and the fraction of repeats in different genomic function regions. (C) The distribution of phastCons(100way) score of randomly selected sites in pre-mRNA sequences. (D) The precisionrecall curves of linear regression (LR) models for predicting conservative/non-conservative sites based on SpliceBERT, SpliceBERT-human and one-hot encoded nucleotide embeddings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 2\n\nFigure  2. SpliceBERT  enables  unsupervised  splice-disrupting  variant  prediction.    The  relationship  between  the  median  value  of  KLdivergence between the MLM logits of wild-type sequences and mutant sequences and the distances to variants in (A) conservative and nonconservative  sites  sampled  by  in  silico  mutagenesis,  (B)  VexSeq  SDV  (splice-disrupting  variant)  and  non-SDV  (non-splice-disrupting variant)  group, and (C) MFASS SDV and non-SDV group. The boxplot of delta-logit ( logit), the sum of KL-divergence in neighboring nucleotides  (KL-div(context))  and  the  KL-divergence  at  splice  donors/acceptors  (KL-div(SS))  of  the  SDV  and  non-SDV  group  in  (D) VexSeq and (E) MFASS. The precision-recall curves of SpliceBERT and baseline methods for classifying SDV and non-SDV samples in (F) VexSeq and (G) MFASS.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 3\n\nFigure  3. UMAP  visualization  of  splice  site  embeddings.  Visualization  of  canonical  splice  sites  and  non-splice  sites  with  GT  and  AG dinucleotides based on (A) SpliceBERT embeddings, (B) DNABERT(k=3) embeddings and (C) one-hot encodings.  Visualization of splice sites  of  high  and  low  usage  in  K562  cell  line  estimated  by  SpliSER  based  on  (D)  SpliceBERT  embeddings,  (E)  DNABERT(k=3) embeddings or (F) one-hot encodings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 4\n\nFigure 4. Analysis of the attention weights in SpliceBERT. (A) The distribution of attention weights between donors and acceptors from the same intron (D-A(intron)), the same exon (D-A(exon)), different intron/exon (D-A(unpair)), between donors and donors (D-D) and between acceptors and acceptors (A-A). A control group of randomly paired nuelcotides is also shown (random pair). The average attention weights across  all  the  6  layers  in  SpliceBERT  were  used.  The  statistical  significance  was  assessed  with  t-test. (B) The  distribution  of phastCons(100way) scores around donors and acceptors. The average value and standard error of the conservations scores in 1000 randomly selected donor-acceptor pairs (from the same introns) are shown. (C) The distribution of donors' and acceptor's atteiton around acceptors and  donors,  respectively,  in  each  Transformer  layer.  The  median  attention  weights  across  different  samples  were  used.  (D-A:  donoracceptor, D-D: donor-donor, A-A: acceptor-acceptor, L: layer. ***: P-value &lt; )\n\nFigure 5. SpliceBERT predicts splice sites in different species. (A) The F1-score of SpliceBERT and baseline models on 5 different species. The visualization of the impact of in silico mutagenesis variants on splice site scores for (B) donors and (D) acceptors and corresponding sequence logos of wild-type sequences (C and E).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 6\n\nFigure 6. SpliceBERT predicts human branchpoint sites. (A) The distribution of high-confidence branchpoints in the Mercer et al dataset. (B) The UMAP visualization of branchpoint and non-branchpoint site embeddings (only adenine sites are shown). (C) The precision-recall curves of the models for predicting branchpoints. (D) The impact of variants at different sites on SpliceBERT predicted BP scores and (E) the corresponding logos of wild-type sequences. (F) The predicted BP score change induced by different types of mutations at BP sites and their 2nt- and 3nt- upstream.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n[input_representation: Self-supervised learning on millions of pre-mRNA sequences improves sequence-based RNA splicing prediction > The SpliceBERT model]\nPre-training  data We  extracted  the  pre-mRNA  sequences  from  72  vertebrate  genomes  for  pre-training.  The  reference  genomes  in  fasta format and gene annotations in gtf format were downloaded from UCSC genome browser (21). The versions of the genomes and annotations are available in Table S1. The pre-mRNA sequences were extracted from genome sequences using 'bedtools getfasta' (22) based on gene annotations and overlapping transcripts were merged. In this way, we obtained a total number of 2011517 pre-mRNA sequences covering about  65  billion  nucleotides  (nt).  We  randomly  reserved  50000  sequences  for  validation  and  pre-trained  SpliceBERT  using  the  other sequences.\n\nModel  architecture We  adopted  the  BERT  architecture  (14)  to  build  SpliceBERT.  SpliceBERT  is  consisted  of  6  Transformer  encoder layers,  where  the  hidden  layer  size  and  the  number  of  attention  heads  are  512  and  16,  respectively.  The  position  information  of  the nucleotides in each sequence are encoded using the one-hot positional embedding method. SpliceBERT has a total of about 19.4 million parameters.\n\nTokenization Previous pre-trained models for DNA sequences mainly adopted the k-mer tokenization (19, 20, 23) or the Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) tokenization (24) strategy. However, in this study, we simply used each nucleotide type (A, G, C, T/U) as a single token for the ease of getting embeddings for individual nucleotides. The [CLS] token (classification) and [SEP] token (separator) were added to the beginning and the end of each sequence input to SpliceBERT, which is a routine operation in most BERT models. The [MASK] token was used to represent the masked tokens in MLM task.\n\nPre-training by masked language modelling task We pre-trained SpliceBERT by the masked language modelling (MLM) task, which is a self-supervised  learning  strategy  to  pre-train  language  models.  Specifically,  for  each  input  sequence,  we  randomly  picked  15%  of  the nucleotides and 80%, 10%, 10% of which were replaced with the special token '[MASK]', random nucleotides, and original nucleotides (they  were  kept  unchanged),  respectively.  During  pre-training,  the  model  was  optimized  to  predict  the  correct  base  type  of  the  original nucleotides.  Intuitively, the  model  was  required  to  learn the  dependencies between  the  nucleotides in the input sequences  to recover the corrupted tokens.  In this way, we expected the model could capture the complex logic that may be encoded in the DNA/RNA sequences. Model implementation and pre-training We implemented SpliceBERT in PyTorch with the Huggingface transformer library (v4.24.0). The maximum length of input sequences was set to 1024. Since most pre-mRNA sequences are longer than 1024nt, fragments no longer than 1024nt  were  randomly  sampled  for  pre-training.  We  used  the  cross-entropy  loss  as  the  objective  to  optimize  the  model.  The  AdamW optimizer  was  used  and  the  initial  learning  rate  was  set  to  0.0001.  The  learning  rate  will  be  reduced  by  a  factor  of  2  when  model's performance on the validation data stops improving for 3 epochs. The model was trained in the automatic mixed precision (AMP) mode to decrease the consumption of memory and speed up model training. In practice, we pre-trained SpliceBERT in two stages. In the first stage, we used fixed-length sequences of 510nt to train SpliceBERT on 8 V100 GPUs for a week and the accuracy of MLM task finally reached about 0.56. Then, we continued to pre-train SpliceBERT with sequences ranging from 64nt to 1024nt until the MLM accuracy reached 0.56 again."}]

